Witty-Accounting is an app to support account energy consumption for
charging an electric car using Hager Witty Wallboxes. 

It is available form GitHub under MIT License.

Data input is done by exporting the charging events into an *.xlsx file
and importing it into Witty-Accounting. With the data it will look up 
the RFID cards and the assigned persons and prepares an invoice for the
charging events.

When the Hager API for electromobility becomes available, direct synchronization
of charging events will be integrate.

If besides grid electricity photovoltaics are available, the energy produced
by it can be priced with a different amount.

The app includes admin pages for user, charging events and invoices. Users can
view own charging events, view and download invoices.

Finalized invoice PDFs can also be exported manually by an administrator to
an SFTP server. The connection data is configured on the settings page. The
private client key and the SSH host-key list stay outside the database and are
mounted read-only from `/srv/witty/secrets`:

```text
/srv/witty/secrets/invoice-export-key
/srv/witty/secrets/invoice-export-known-hosts
```

Both files must be readable by the container user configured through
`WITTY_UID` and `WITTY_GID`; the private key should use mode `0600`.

The public client key must be installed for the configured account on the
SFTP server. Create the known-hosts file with `ssh-keyscan`, for example:

```bash
ssh-keyscan -p 22 sftp.example.org \
  > /srv/witty/secrets/invoice-export-known-hosts
```

Verify the displayed fingerprint independently before enabling the export.
The file locations and connection timeout can be changed in `.env` with
`INVOICE_EXPORT_SFTP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH`,
`INVOICE_EXPORT_SFTP_KNOWN_HOSTS_PATH` and
`INVOICE_EXPORT_SFTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`.

Data ist stored in a volume atted to a MariaDB container. On rebuils reuse the
data container to keep all data.

The Witty app is in German.


INSTALLTION
===========
The Witty-Accounting file are stored in GitHub at https://github.com/klpgo/Witty-Accounting


Prerequisites
-------------
Witty-Accounting was developed on Debian. You need a working docker environment 
including docker-buildx- and docker-compose-plugins and git to access the source
code from the repository. In addition it uses ca-certificates and curl.

Check your environment by issuing thes commands:
- git --version
- docker --version
- docker buildx version
- docker compose version

The build system must have access to the internet load neccessary software packages.


Check out the files from Git
----------------------------
$ git clone https://github.com/klpgo/Witty-Accounting


Edit file .env
--------------
The .env file provides neccessary information to the build 
process. There is an example .env-example enclosed in the Git archive

$ mv .env-example .env
$ vi .env


Build and Run Witty-Accounting 
------------------------------
$ docker compose build
$ docker compose up -d


Add the first Admin User
------------------------
Use the following command to create the first admin user for the app.
This user is able to connect to the app and configure the remaining
settings using the settings page.

$ docker compose run --rm --no-deps \
  -v "$PWD/backend/scripts:/app/scripts:ro" \
  backend python -m scripts.create_admin

Only the first admin user can be created using this command. Other
users (including other admins) are created though the user page.


Access the App
--------------
The App Services are provided on port 8000 on the docker host.
Use your browser to access the Witty-Accounting app by pointin 
it to http://<docker host>:8000.


Multi-tenant operation
----------------------
The operating mode is selected through TENANCY_ENABLED during setup.
With TENANCY_ENABLED=false Witty runs as a standalone installation and
uses the configured database and invoice archive directly. With
TENANCY_ENABLED=true Witty uses one shared frontend and backend with a
separate MariaDB database and invoice archive subdirectory for every
tenant. The tenant is selected through the request hostname.

The preparation and activation procedure is documented in
docs/multi-tenancy.md.


Local tenant administration
---------------------------
The separate `witty-control` service manages tenants and is bound only to the
server loopback address at `http://127.0.0.1:8001`. Add a long control password
and an independent session secret (at least 32 characters) to `.env`:

```dotenv
WITTY_CONTROL_PASSWORD=<long-random-password>
WITTY_CONTROL_SESSION_SECRET=<independent-random-secret>
WITTY_CONTROL_SESSION_MINUTES=30
WITTY_CONTROL_PORT=8001
```

Build and start it with:

```bash
docker compose --env-file .env build control
docker compose --env-file .env up -d control
```

For a remote server use an SSH tunnel and then open
`http://127.0.0.1:8001` locally:

```bash
ssh -L 8001:127.0.0.1:8001 <server>
```

Creating a tenant creates its database, a random database password, all schema
migrations and the first administrator. Deleting a tenant permanently removes
its registry entry, database, database user and invoice archive. Create an
external backup first; this deletion cannot be undone.
